      "headline": "The Four Foundations: A Toolset for Living",
      "keyInsight": "People often treat meditation like a vague mystical fog where you float into space. Bhikkhu Analayo shows that Satipatthana is actually a structured diagnostic kit with four distinct gauges. Think of a pilot checking an instrument panel before takeoff. You do not just stare into the clouds and hope for the best. You check your altitude, fuel, engine heat, and radar. The four foundations are your body, your basic feelings, your mental mood, and the patterns running your life. The counterintuitive secret is that these four areas are not separate islands. When your posture slumps, your mood dims, and sour thoughts instantly multiply. By scanning all four gauges regularly, you catch mental drift miles before an engine stalls. You move from blind turbulence into calm navigation.",
      "content": "Think of setting up a solid four-legged table in a workshop. If you only build one leg, the whole surface wobbles and drops your tools. Many people try to meditate by only monitoring their thoughts. They ignore their tight shoulders, their racing pulse, and the sour taste of irritation. Satipatthana gives you four solid legs: the physical body, the immediate pleasant or unpleasant tone, the state of the mind, and the natural laws of cause and effect.\n\nWhen you sit down, you start with the most obvious anchor: your physical form. You feel your feet on the floor, your back against the chair, and your breath moving smoothly. Next, you notice the simple gut reaction to every moment. Is this feeling pleasant, painful, or neutral? If you ignore that basic switch, your mind instantly turns pleasant feelings into craving and unpleasant feelings into anger.\n\nThen, you check the overall weather of your mind. Is your mental sky cloudy with fatigue or buzzing with nervous electricity? Finally, you look at underlying patterns and habits. You see how a restless impulse rises, stays briefly, and naturally fades away. You are no longer caught in the drama. You are watching a fascinating laboratory experiment unfold from your armchair.",
