      "headline": "Anatomy: Unpacking the Physical Suit",
      "keyInsight": "We spend billions of dollars pampering, decorating, and worrying about our physical appearance. We view the body as a single shiny statue that defines who we are. Bhikkhu Analayo unpacks the ancient practice of looking straight under the hood. Imagine buying an exotic luxury car and thinking the sleek red paint is the entire machine. If you open the hood, you see hoses, spark plugs, belts, grease, and radiator coolant. None of those individual parts look glamorous on their own, but together they run the vehicle. Looking at your body as an assembly of bones, tissues, muscles, and liquids destroys vanity without creating hate. It frees you from the exhausting trap of endless body comparison. You stop worshiping the chassis and simply take good care of the engine.",
      "content": "Take a look at your watch or your smartphone. On the outside, it looks like a sleek, seamless piece of polished glass and metal. But if you take a screwdriver and open the back casing, you see tiny screws, ribbons of wire, and battery cells. You do not get angry at the battery or worship the screws. You recognize that the device is simply a collection of functional components working together in harmony.\n\nIn this chapter, Analayo guides you to view your own body with that same cool, objective lens. We usually view our bodies through a haze of social anxiety, pride, or self-criticism. We obsess over a gray hair, a wrinkle, or the shape of our nose. Mindfulness of anatomy cuts through this spell. You scan your bones, muscles, skin, blood, and vital organs just like an engineer surveying a machine.\n\nThis simple shift brings immense psychological relief. When you see your knee as cartilage and bone instead of 'my failing body', pain loses its emotional sting. You care for the body like a trusted vehicle that carries you from town to town. You feed it, wash it, and let it rest, completely free from the exhausting burden of vanity.",
