      "headline": "The Expiration Date on the Milk Carton",
      "keyInsight": "Modern life spends billions pretending death is an unexpected glitch in the system. We hide hospitals away, dye our graying hair, and draft plans for decades ahead. We act like renters who believe they own the apartment complex outright. Analayo points out that ancient meditators did something counterintuitive. They walked straight out to the town graveyard to sit beside decaying bones. Why? Because looking honestly at the finish line instantly cures life's silly dramas. When you buy a fresh carton of milk, you do not throw a tantrum because it has an expiration date. You simply drink your morning coffee and appreciate it today. Remembering that your body will eventually dissolve into compost is not depressing. It is the ultimate wake-up call. It washes away petty jealousies, useless arguments, and endless digital scrolling. Death is the quiet frame that gives the painting of your life vivid color.",
      "content": "Think about renting a scenic cabin in the mountains for a weekend getaway. You know Monday morning checkout is coming at ten sharp. Because your stay is brief, you do not spend Saturday repainting the walls or arguing over scratches on the porch floor. You sit quietly outside, sip hot tea, and marvel at the sunrise.\n\nContemplating death operates on the exact same logic. In mindfulness practice, observing our mortality is not a morbid fascination with gloom. It is an honest inspection of your rental agreement with nature. Every physical form eventually wears out, breaks down, and returns its borrowed parts.\n\nWhen we refuse to face this fact, we live in constant low-grade dread. We panic at minor illnesses and obsess over fading youth. But when you look directly at life's final checkout time, a funny lightness appears. You forgive small annoyances much faster. You pick up the phone to tell old friends you love them.\n\nDeath meditation is simply turning on the lights in a dark closet. Once you see the expiration date clearly stamped on the label, you finally start living with your whole heart.",
