      "headline": "The Traffic Light in Your Mind",
      "keyInsight": "Right now, your brain is running a non-stop grading service. The second any sight, sound, or thought pops up, your mind slaps an instant sticker on it: pleasant, unpleasant, or neutral. This split-second reaction is what ancient psychology calls feeling tone. It is not an emotion like anger or joy yet. It is the raw green, red, or yellow light that flashes first. Here is the trap: we treat these flashes as commands. A pleasant green light flashes, and we chase it like an eager dog chasing a ball. A prickly red light flashes, and we swat at it with irritation. A dull yellow light flashes, and we zone out completely. We let simple physical sensations drive our entire emotional life. By catching the signal at the raw traffic light stage, you gain a brand-new superpower. You can feel a tiny pinch of discomfort without throwing a mental tantrum.",
      "content": "Imagine driving your car through a bustling city intersection. When a traffic light turns red, you do not climb out and scream at the pole. You simply step on the brake, wait patiently, and watch cross traffic pass by. The colored light is just basic information guiding your commute.\n\nYet inside our heads, we act like frantic drivers at every single intersection. When a pleasant taste hits our mouth, we desperately crave five more bites. When a coworker makes a slightly sharp remark, our mind flashes bright red, and we instantly spin a dramatic revenge monologue. When routine work feels plain, we mindlessly pull out a phone to hunt for cheap digital dopamine.\n\nAnalayo teaches us to zoom in on this exact friction point. Notice the bare physical feeling before your storytelling brain takes the wheel. An itch on your knee is just a mild prickly sensation. It is not an emergency, and it is not an insult.\n\nWhen you separate the raw feeling from your knee-jerk reaction, peace moves in. You give yourself room to pause, breathe, and choose your next turn with cool wisdom.",
